<p>This <strong>Cubii Move review</strong> answers the question most shoppers have: is the lighter, cheaper Cubii good enough, or should you spend more on the JR2? The Cubii Move is a manual seated elliptical built to be picked up and carried between rooms. You pedal it in your chair, it runs quietly, and it needs no power.</p>
<p>The trade-off is simple. A lighter machine is easier to move but also easier to push across the floor, and the Move has less resistance range than the heavier JR2. If you want one Cubii that follows you from the desk to the couch, the Move makes sense. If it will sit under one desk forever, the JR2 is the stronger buy. Our verdicts here are based on specs and owner reviews.</p>

<h2>What Is the Cubii Move?</h2>
<p>The Cubii Move is part of Cubii's family of seated ellipticals, alongside the JR1, JR2, JR2+ and GO. It uses the same basic design: two pedals that travel in a smooth oval, an adjustable resistance setting and a small display for your session stats. There is no app and no Bluetooth.</p>
<p>What sets it apart is weight and price. Cubii positions it as the one you can lift with one hand and carry from the office to the living room, at a lower price point than the JR2. Check the [[amazon:cubii-move|current Cubii Move price on Amazon]], since it moves around with sales.</p>

<h2>Cubii Move Review: The Good</h2>
<h3>Easy to carry and store</h3>
<p>This is the main reason to choose it. If you work at a desk during the day and want to pedal in front of the TV at night, a light machine removes the friction of moving it. Owners who share one machine between rooms tend to rate it highly for exactly this.</p>
<h3>Quiet enough for calls</h3>
<p>Like other Cubii models, the Move is quiet in normal use. You can pedal through a video meeting without the hum of a motor or the squeak of a cheap friction strap. If silence is your top priority, compare it with the models in our <a href="/quietest-under-desk-elliptical/">quietest under desk elliptical</a> guide.</p>
<h3>Gentle, low-impact motion</h3>
<p>The elliptical path is kind to knees and hips, which makes it an easy machine for beginners and older users. You can start at a low setting and keep going for long stretches without feeling sore.</p>
<h3>Lower entry price for the Cubii name</h3>
<p>If you want Cubii's support and build quality but not the top-end price, the Move is one of the cheaper ways into the lineup.</p>

<h2>Cubii Move Reviews: The Common Complaints</h2>
<p>Read enough Cubii Move reviews and the same few issues come up. None are surprising for a light seated elliptical, and most are fixable.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>It slides on hard floors.</strong> A lighter base has less grip. A [[amazon:cubii-mat|non-slip mat]] solves this for most people, and bracing the machine against a desk leg helps too.</li>
<li><strong>The chair rolls away.</strong> If your office chair has casters, every push moves the chair. [[amazon:caster-cups|Caster cups]] lock it in place.</li>
<li><strong>Resistance tops out early.</strong> Fit users find the highest setting easy. That is a design choice: the Move is for steady movement, not a sweaty workout.</li>
<li><strong>Knee clearance at low desks.</strong> As with any seated elliptical, your knees rise at the top of the stride. Measure your desk first.</li>
</ul>
