<h3>Cubii Move vs Cubii GO</h3>
<p>The GO is the more compact of the two, with a built-in display and a shorter stride. It is the one to pick if the space under your desk is tight or you want something to toss in the car. The Move feels more like a full-size Cubii while still being easy to lift. If you will mostly use it at home, the Move is the better all-rounder. If you travel or work in a small space, the [[amazon:cubii-go|Cubii GO]] makes more sense.</p>
<h3>Cubii Move vs JR2</h3>
<p>The JR2 is Cubii's benchmark model. It is heavier, which keeps it planted and gives it a smoother feel at higher resistance. If you will leave the machine under one desk, the extra weight is a benefit, not a drawback. Pay the difference for the JR2 unless portability is a real need.</p>
<h3>Cubii Move vs JR1</h3>
<p>The JR1 is the original budget Cubii. It is also manual, quiet and app-free, but with a slightly older design. Both sit at the lower end of the price range, so compare the two listings on the day you buy. Our <a href="/cubii-jr1-under-desk-elliptical/">Cubii JR1 guide</a> covers when the older model is still worth it.</p>
<h3>What Cubii GO reviews say</h3>
<p>Since many people researching the Move also read Cubii GO reviews, here is the pattern. Owners like how little room the GO takes and how easy it is to pack for a trip or stash in a cupboard. The complaints mirror its design: the stride feels short to taller users, and the resistance suits gentle movement more than effort. People who bought it as a travel or second machine tend to be happiest. People who bought it as their only machine sometimes wish they had chosen a full-size Cubii.</p>
