<h2>How Many Calories Can You Burn on a Cubii Move?</h2>
<p>Expect a modest burn. Light seated pedaling is roughly 2.0 METs in the Compendium of Physical Activities, compared with about 1.3 for sitting still. For most people that works out to around 100 to 150 calories an hour at an easy pace, and more at higher resistance or body weight. Pedaling for an hour or two spread across a workday adds up over a month.</p>
<p>Plug in your own weight and minutes with our <a href="/tools/under-desk-elliptical-calorie-calculator/">calorie calculator</a>, and see the full picture in <a href="/does-under-desk-elliptical-burn-calories/">does an under desk elliptical burn calories</a>.</p>

<h2>Setup Tips for a Light Seated Elliptical</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Put down a mat first.</strong> It stops sliding, protects floors and dampens any vibration.</li>
<li><strong>Lock your chair.</strong> A chair without casters, or casters in cups, keeps your pushes going into the pedals.</li>
<li><strong>Sit slightly further back.</strong> This gives your knees more room under the desk and keeps your back against the chair.</li>
<li><strong>Start low and short.</strong> Fifteen-minute blocks at light resistance are easy to repeat. Our <a href="/tools/desk-pedal-timer/">desk pedal timer</a> can prompt you.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Verdict: Is the Cubii Move Worth It?</h2>
<p>Our Cubii Move review lands here: it is a good buy for one specific person, the one who wants a quiet Cubii they can carry from room to room. For that job, its light build is the whole point. For everyone else, the heavier [[amazon:cubii-jr2|Cubii JR2]] is smoother, stays put better and has more resistance to grow into.</p>
<p>If space or travel is your concern, look at the GO. If you want data, look at the JR2+. Whichever Cubii you pick, budget for a mat, because a seated elliptical that stays put is one you will keep using. And if you are still unsure, the <a href="/tools/which-under-desk-elliptical-quiz/">60-second quiz</a> will match you to a model.</p>
